								<div class="title" id="post-title-477595">
																		Squaring a velocity is meaningless
															</div>
				<div class="info">
					<span class="printuser avatarhover"><a href="http://www.wikidot.com/user:info/beedee" onclick="WIKIDOT.page.listeners.userInfo(324750); return false;"  ><img class="small" src="http://www.wikidot.com/avatar.php?userid=324750&amp;amp;size=small&amp;amp;timestamp=1608954469" alt="BeeDee" style="background-image:url(http://www.wikidot.com/userkarma.php?u=324750)"/></a><a href="http://www.wikidot.com/user:info/beedee" onclick="WIKIDOT.page.listeners.userInfo(324750); return false;" >BeeDee</a></span> <span class="odate time_1242178229 format_%25e%20%25b%20%25Y%2C%20%25H%3A%25M%7Cagohover">13 May 2009 01:30</span>
														</div>
			</div>
			<div class="content" id="post-content-477595">
									

<p>There's a bit of a problem, though. You can't "square" something's velocity, it's nonsensical. For example, if it's moving at 2 meters per second, you might expect it to "square" to 4 meters per second. But if it's moving at 2 meters per second it's also moving at 7.2 kilometers per hour. So it would "square" to 51.84 kilometers per hour, which is 14.4 meters per second. The velocity changes completely depending on which units you arbitrarily use. If you pick units where the velocity is less than 1 squaring it actually <em>reduces</em> the velocity.</p>
<p>There are a few SCPs out there where this sort of thing is actually addressed, such as SCP-233, but that sort of weirdness should be explicitly mentioned.</p>

							</div>

								
					
